							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">&quot;This is a response to dawson&#039;s posts both here and in the other thread about the FBI investigation.&nbsp; Terrorism is just a smoke screen.&nbsp; The administration is merely using it as a replacement threat for Communism, to keep the masses in check and justify their actions.&nbsp; The most celebrated act of terrorism perpetrated in this country before 5 years ago was the Boston Tea Party.&nbsp; The most notorious was the genocide of the hundreds of thousands of people already living here.&nbsp; I&#039;m sure you can guess what the most profitable one was.&nbsp; The examples go on and on, through to this very day.&nbsp; In my mind, just because an act is perpetuated by a Government, doesn&#039;t mean it&#039;s not a terrorist act.&nbsp; I could sit here and type out some long diatribe about it (and some might say I already have),&nbsp; but hip-hop comes to the rescue in the form of a song with a video (lyrics and some pics are worth 10,000 words), and it&#039;s only one of many examples which could illustrate the point.&quot;<br /><br />i love how you can say definitively that &quot;terrorism is a smoke screen&quot; and nobody here challenges you in the slightest. right now you guys are playing the role of Fox News liberals, people who complain about nothing and in turn discredit valid progressive protest. the Boston Tea Party wasn&#039;t a vehicle for intimidation or coersion through fear, so i can&#039;t agree that it was an act of terrorism. the killing of Native Americans was horrible but certainly doesn&#039;t fall under the category of terrorism since the European murderers&#039; crimes benefited them directly as opposed to influencing the so-called Indians by engineering fear. also.....these acts were commited before the government EXISTED. you have good intentions but you&#039;re just rambling on about bad stuff white people have done and pretending that it&#039;s a structured argument. i&#039;ve seen that video a million times and agree with the general message but i&#039;ve lived a cushy life and i&#039;ve been hit by a lot worse than a series of questions. i agree that the government employs its own brand of terrorism (even discounting the fact that they might&#039;ve engineered 9/11), convincing the public of the need for their policies by threatening them with their Saudi Arabian puppet, but i think this particular incident represents one of the most promising manifestations of the powers granted by the Patriot Act. if the government wishes to go through all of that sinister plotting just to ask a dude some questions, they can go ahead and do so because annoyance&gt;anarchy. detaining people (which they have done) is bad, bothering isn&#039;t a big deal. i&#039;m not saying that governmental micromanagement and oppression is fine and dandy, i&#039;m saying that you&#039;re being a little bit megalomaniacal if you think some interrogative questions are sufficiently evil that you need to compare them to the slaughter of Native Americans.<br /><br />&quot;Don&#039;t let the Fourth Estate do your thinking for you, &#039;cause you seem to be approaching the situation with an open and reasoned mind, however you are stuck on the idea that these transgressions are necessary to maintain the safety of the people.&nbsp; When in point of fact, in five years of this sort of monitoring, they&#039;ve arrested a group of guys who couldn&#039;t afford boots, some other guys who were stockpiling cellphones, and one other group who &#039;possibly&#039; were a sleeper cell.&nbsp; Whom you never hear about conclusively, because that would compromise the &quot;War on Terror&quot;.&quot;<br /><br />yes i am stuck on that idea. i do believe that the government needs to know what is going on within the country to protect the country. damn, i must be crazy. &quot;in five years of this sort of monitoring, they&#039;ve arrested a group of guys who couldn&#039;t afford boots, some other guys who were stockpiling cellphones, and one other group who &#039;possibly&#039; were a sleeper cell.&quot; hahaha i love this. a conspiracy theorist like yourself is of the opinion that the government publicizes all of these arrests.<br /><br />&quot;Long story short; know your history to more fully understand what is happening at the present time.<br /><br />Anyway check this video Immortal Technique - The 4th Branch. <br />Internalize the information and adhere to the admonishment at the end.&quot;<br /><br />i don&#039;t adhere to what people tell me to do. whether it&#039;s oppressive government or oppressive paranoia, i&#039;m going to stick with it to the degree that makes sense to me, not necessarily the degree to which it makes sense to Dick Cheney or to MC Geologic.<br /><br />&quot;As someone else pointed out, the first thing I thought upon posting was &quot;The FBI are probably going to read this,&quot;&nbsp; this, also, is terror.&quot;<br /><br />oh, shit. they&#039;re gonna read some shit posted on a public forum? damn, you must feel important.<br /><br />&quot;peace dawson. what &quot;filipino terrorist&quot; organizations do you know about that has ever posed any danger to you or this country? if you know of any, i&#039;d like to hear about it. plus, nobody has said or is saying that we&#039;re being targeted primarily because we&#039;re filipino. nobody. where did you get that idea?&quot;<br /><br />somebody else used the word &quot;racist&quot;. i was probably too quick in assuming that they were tying that adjective to this particular situation. somebody mentioned al-qaeda linked organizations in the phillipines. when i said that i was mostly speaking hypothetically, anyway.<br /><br />&quot;ditto fotav. there is a long history of the US government monitoring and blacklisting progressive filipino americans, from labor organizers in the 40&#039;s and 50&#039;s such as carlos bulosan to the KDP in the 70&#039;s and 80&#039;s. i&#039;d suggest picking up a book like The Cointelpro Papers or anything that highlights the history of the FBI and CIA. the whole national security thing is a guise to disrupt political dissenters. oldest ruling class tactic in the book, and right now you&#039;re falling for it.&quot;<br /><br />i&#039;d rather fall for that than fall for whatever misdirection led to 9/11. plus, you&#039;re being classist yourself in assuming that it&#039;s a front. i admit that i agree with the sentiment that the government has strong alterior motives for their policies, but what i&#039;m trying to communicate is the fact that this monitoring isn&#039;t a bad thing unless something more serious comes of it. they can monitor all they want and as long as they don&#039;t do anything harmful, what bothers you about having an auditor?<br /><br />&quot;free&#039;s case is not due to &quot;racial profiling&quot; but cuz he criticizes us imperialism (aka &quot;globalization&quot;) which is a red flag for authorities if you actually in the community talking about it...<br /><br />the feds don&#039;t care if you are critical of us policy in academia, but get scurred (historically) once folks start organizing the community around it...&quot;<br /><br />you&#039;re ignorant if you think globalization is synonymous with imperialism. some parts of it are but globalization is far too diverse to make such gross generalizations about. i reccomend reading &quot;globaloney&quot;. it serves to expel some of the myths (good and bad) about globalization. entertaining read, too.<br /><br />&quot;all adds up to we need to have each other&#039;s backs. and the government is gonna do it&#039;s thing, but that doesn&#039;t mean it will do it honestly, or that the outcomes are in most people&#039;s interests.<br /><br />not saying terrorism isn&#039;t bad, just saying that the US govt usually aggrivates it rather than stops it.&quot;<br /><br />i agree completely. thank you for using phrases like &quot;most people&quot; and &quot;usually&quot;.</div>
						</td>
