							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">September 29, 2006, the US Senate agreed to the Military Commissions Act of 2006 which gives US President George Bush unprecedented power to detain and try people as part of their “War on Terror.” President Bush is then expected to sign the Act into law. Broadly, the new Act does 3 things:<br /><br />&nbsp; &nbsp;1. Strips the right of detainees to habeas corpus (the traditional right of detainees to challenge their detention);<br />&nbsp; &nbsp;2. Gives the US President the power to detain indefinitely anyone—US or foreign nationals, from within the US, and from abroad—it deems to have provided material support to anti-US hostilities, and even use secret and coerced evidence (i.e. through use of torture) to try detainees who will be held in secret US military prisons;<br />&nbsp; &nbsp;3. Gives US officials immunity from prosecution for torturing detainees that were captured before the end of 2005 by US military and CIA.</div><br />There are many organizations in the US and around the world that are linked up with legal activist and political organizations in the Philippines, providing clothing donations, computers, etc. These organizations are completely legal under the Philippine constitution, and are made up of students, labor unionists, church leaders, human rights advocates, peasants, journalists, teachers, lawyers, etcetera. These organizations are strongly critical of the Philippine administration and policies, as well as the US administration and policies.&nbsp; <b>These organizations are legal and protected by Philippine law, yet since 2001 more than 750 members of these organizations have been the targets of political killings with the Armed Forces of the Philippines and linked paramilitary groups as the primary suspects.</b> The inaction from the Philippine government regarding prevention or investigation of these killings leads many to believe it is sectioned from the very top, President Gloria Macapagal-Arroyo. The US currently has about 4000 troops stationed in the Philippines, who are training the Filipino soldiers in &quot;anti-insurgency&quot; tactics. The US govt and Philippine govt walk together lock step. Let me repeat that more than 750 Filipino activists, critical of the Arroyo administration and its policies, have been murdered in the last five years.<br /><br />Legal activists in the Philippines are the target of fascist state repression by their government, they are being covertly treated as the enemy of the state even though their actions are protected by Filipino law. Now, legal activists in the US who have a history of supporting legal activists in the Philippines are being targeted for investigation. And just two weeks ago the congress passes a law that allows indefinite detention and torture of those who may have provided material support to anti-US causes. <br /><br />Now, those of us involved in supporting the activist work in the Philippines shouldn&#039;t be worried because our actions here are completely legal, and the action we support in the Philippines are completely legal. But, 750+ legal activists have been murdered in the Philippines. And now organizers are being investigated in the US. What is next? That&#039;s what scares me.<br /><br />For more info:<br /><a href="http&#58;//www.bayanusa.org" target="_blank">www.bayanusa.org</a><br /><a href="http&#58;//www.stopthekillings.org" target="_blank">www.stopthekillings.org</a><br /><a href="http&#58;//www.bulatlat.com" target="_blank">www.bulatlat.com</a><br /></div>
